---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What priority should my MX records use?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Lower numbers are tried first. Use 10 for the primary and 20 or higher for backups; equal priorities split load instead of failing over.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I have multiple MX records?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes, and it is the standard resilience pattern: two or three hosts at different priorities.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How long before MX changes work?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'As long as the old record TTL. Lower it to 300 seconds before the change, and the new routing applies within minutes.' } },
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="How to Add MX Records for Email" description="How to add MX records step by step: get your mail provider's values, publish with correct priorities, avoid the classic mistakes, and verify."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["Add MX Records"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Add MX Records</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Add MX Records</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      MX records route your incoming mail. Adding them is five minutes of careful copying from your
      mail provider's instructions; the failure modes are all predictable.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#step-by-step">Step by step</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-classic-mistakes">The classic mistakes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#after-the-mx-complete-receiving">After the MX: complete receiving</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="step-by-step">Step by step</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Open your mail provider's DNS instructions.</strong> Google Workspace, M365, Zoho, Proton all publish their exact MX hosts and priorities. Copy, do not retype.</li>
        <li><strong>In your DNS host, create MX records</strong> on the root domain. Each line gets: host <code>@</code>, type MX, priority (10, 20, ...), value the mail hostname with its trailing dot if your host uses FQDNs.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove old MX records completely.</strong> Mixed old and new sets mean some mail lands on the old server forever.</li>
        <li><strong>Lower expectations, not TTLs.</strong> If migrating providers with users live, pre-set TTL 300 the day before so the cutover is minutes, not a day.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify.</strong> The <a href="/dns-record-lookup/">record lookup</a> with MX selected shows your new set from a public resolver; the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> confirms every resolver agrees before you decommission the old host.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="the-classic-mistakes">The classic mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Pointing MX at an IP address; MX takes a hostname, always.</li>
        <li>Priority confusion: lower number = higher priority; equal numbers load-balance.</li>
        <li>Adding a new provider's MX while keeping the old ones "just in case": mail splits unpredictably.</li>
        <li>Forgetting the subdomains: <code>mail.yourdomain.com</code> needs its own MX if it receives directly.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="after-the-mx-complete-receiving">After the MX: complete receiving</h2>
      <p>
        MX routes mail, but deliverability of your <em>outbound</em> mail needs SPF, DKIM, and DMARC.
        The homepage <a href="/">scanner</a> checks the whole stack in one pass.
      </p>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/mx-record/">MX records explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/mx-record-not-working/">MX not working? Diagnosis</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What priority should my MX records use?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Lower numbers are tried first. Use 10 for the primary and 20 or higher for backups; equal priorities split load instead of failing over.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I have multiple MX records?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, and it is the standard resilience pattern: two or three hosts at different priorities.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long before MX changes work?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">As long as the old record TTL. Lower it to 300 seconds before the change, and the new routing applies within minutes.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
